"Window: \"Flagstone: Sky Halisky\", App: Google Chrome for Testing.\n0 standard window Flagstone: Sky Halisky - Google Chrome for Testing, URL: 127.0.0.1:4405/work/flagstone, Secondary Actions: Raise\n\t1 container Flagstone: Sky Halisky - Google Chrome for Testing, URL: 127.0.0.1:4405/work/flagstone\n\t\t2 container\n\t\t\t3 toolbar\n\t\t\t\t4 button Back\n\t\t\t\t5 button (disabled) Forward\n\t\t\t\t6 HTML content chrome://webui-toolbar.top-chrome/\n\t\t\t\t\t7 button Description: Reload, Help: Reload this page\n\t\t\t\t8 container\n\t\t\t\t\t9 pop up button View site information\n\t\t\t\t\t10 text field (settable) Description: Address and search bar, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/work/flagstone, Placeholder: Search Google or type a URL\n\t\t\t\t\t11 button Zoom: 200%\n\t\t\t\t\t12 button Bookmark this tab\n\t\t\t\t13 button You\n\t\t\t\t14 pop up button Chromium\n\t\t\t15 HTML content Flagstone: Sky Halisky, URL: 127.0.0.1:4405/work/flagstone\n\t\t\t\t16 link Description: Skip to main content, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/work/flagstone#main\n\t\t\t\t17 button Open navigation menu\n\t\t\t\t18 container\n\t\t\t\t\t19 container Breadcrumb\n\t\t\t\t\t\t20 content list\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t21 link Description: THE WORK, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/work/\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t22 text FLAGSTONE\n\t\t\t\t\t23 image Flagstone's Explore map with an expanded verified Steep grade report, a real barrier photograph, orange map pins, map controls, and bottom navigation.\n\t\t\t\t\t24 text SEVERITY 3 OF 5 · MODERATE · VERIFIED\n\t\t\t\t\t25 text Steep grade: a real barrier report expanded on the map.\n\t\t\t\t\t26 text CURRENT EXPLORE SCREEN · FLAGSTONE\n\t\t\t\t\t27 text FEATURED\n\t\t\t\t\t28 text SOLO BUILDER · AI-ASSISTED\n\t\t\t\t\t29 text SKY HALISKY\n\t\t\t\t\t30 heading Flagstone, Value: 1\n\t\t\t\t\t\t31 text Flagstone\n\t\t\t\t\t32 text Community map of accessibility barriers: report a broken ramp, verify a neighbour's, see it marked fixed. Privacy-first: no ads, no analytics, no trackers.\n\t\t\t\t\t33 link Description: Live demo for Flagstone (opens in new tab), Value: flagstone.skypistudio.com/\n\t\t\t\t\t34 definition list\n\t\t\t\t\t\t35 container STATUS\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t36 text STATUS\n\t\t\t\t\t\t37 text App Store review submitted · August 2026\n\t\t\t\t\t\t38 container ROLE\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t39 text ROLE\n\t\t\t\t\t\t40 text Solo builder · AI-assisted\n\t\t\t\t\t\t41 container YEAR\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t42 text YEAR\n\t\t\t\t\t\t43 text 2026\n\t\t\t\t\t44 text TECH\n\t\t\t\t\t45 content list\n\t\t\t\t\t\t46 text EXPO\n\t\t\t\t\t\t47 text REACT NATIVE\n\t\t\t\t\t\t48 text SUPABASE\n\t\t\t\t\t\t49 text TYPESCRIPT\n\t\t\t\t\t50 text LINKS\n\t\t\t\t\t51 content list\n\t\t\t\t\t\t52 link Description: GitHub (opens in new tab), Value: github.com/Skypie99/AccessMap\n\t\t\t\t\t53 text TAGS\n\t\t\t\t\t54 content list\n\t\t\t\t\t\t55 text ACCESSIBILITY\n\t\t\t\t\t\t56 text MOBILE\n\t\t\t\t\t\t57 text PRIVACY\n\t\t\t\t\t58 container Flagstone case study\n\t\t\t\t\t\t59 heading The problem, Value: 2\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t60 text The problem\n\t\t\t\t\t\t61 text When someone who depends on accessible routes meets a broken ramp or a missing curb cut, reporting it means a municipal form. Describe the problem, submit, wait, hear nothing. Nobody else finds out. There is no way to signal that a barrier is still there, or to confirm that someone fixed it, so one obstacle gets reported by ten people who never learn about each other, and each of them files it from a place they physically go, which is its own disclosure. I met this gap working with the vision loss community\n\t\t\t\t\t\t62 heading The approach, Value: 2\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t63 text The approach\n\t\t\t\t\t\t64 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t65 text I chose React Native and Expo to force myself to learn cross-platform development while shipping something real. Supabase handled auth and the database so I could focus on the domain problem instead of infrastructure. The pivotal call was implementing \n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t66 link Description: Postgres Row Level Security (opens in new tab), Value: github.com/Skypie99/AccessMap/tree/main/supabase/migrations\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t67 text  from day one. Barriers are meant to be seen. A shared map is the whole point, but only the person who filed a report can edit its content; status changes like verifying it or marking it fixed are open to the community that shares the map. That wasn’t an afterthought; it shaped every feature that followed.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t68 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t69 text The hardest part wasn’t the code. My first designs missed what users actually wanted. They didn’t just want to report problems. They wanted to  verify  them, share workarounds, celebrate fixes together. That insight changed everything.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t70 text The map was the other hard part. To a screen reader a native map is one opaque view, so Flagstone keeps a parallel, linear list of nearby barriers that opens on its own when a screen reader is detected, and selecting a marker no longer drags focus back to the map. Text follows Dynamic Type up through the accessibility sizes, touch targets hold a 44-point floor, contrast is unit-tested against the WCAG ratio, and when Reduce Motion is on the map camera jumps instead of animating. The app is built and tested \n\t\t\t\t\t\t71 text Privacy set the remaining shape. Location is foreground-only: the app declares no background-location entitlement and calls no background-location API, a line the privacy review held when an unrelated fix tried to add the keys. Every submission, anonymous or signed in, runs the same content filter, and any flag or comment can be reported through the abuse-report sheet Apple requires. Photos are capped in size and scrubbed before upload. Accounts can be deleted, with the cascade handled by a tested pipeline.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t72 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t73 text Two write paths, not one path with an  anonymous  flag on it: a signed-in report and an anonymous report reach Postgres through separate code, and Row Level Security enforces both. A flag is a value a later read can forget to check; a second path cannot be entered by accident.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t74 heading Where it stands, Value: 2\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t75 text Where it stands\n\t\t\t\t\t\t76 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t77 text A map-first mobile app where accessibility barriers are pinned to real city locations. Report one, verify someone else’s, and follow up when it gets fixed. The \n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t78 link Description: web build (opens in new tab), Value: flagstone.skypistudio.com/\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t79 text  is browsable today. The iOS app has not shipped. It was submitted to Apple for App Store review on August 31, 2026. Before submission, one early TestFlight build reached a single outside tester. Apple approval and public App Store availability have not been established.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t80 text Until that review completes there is no adoption to report, and I would rather say so than imply otherwise. What exists is evidence: a test suite in the thousands, with a dated figure receipted below; a real-device simulator walk that produced 48 findings, 46 of them fixed across four waves and 2 deliberately left open as documented scope calls; and a privacy model whose shape, and the reasons for it, follow.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t81 heading My role, Value: 2\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t82 text My role\n\t\t\t\t\t\t83 text MINE.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t84 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t85 text Anonymous reporting gets its own write path rather than an  anonymous  flag on the shared one. A flag is a value some later code path can forget to read; a second path cannot be entered by accident. The cost is that two paths drift, and they have. I take that trade because drift shows up in review and a missed flag does not.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t86 text The photo scrubber fails closed: if the location data cannot be verified gone, the upload aborts. The alternative was to carry on with the original bytes, which is what an earlier version silently did. The library call it trusted returns nothing at all, so the “stripped” result was always undefined and the untouched buffer went up. For an app where people photograph places they physically go, a false rejection is an annoyance and a false acceptance is a disclosure.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t87 text THE AGENTS’.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t88 text Effectively all of the implementation, the whole test suite, and most of the diagnosis, across more than 1,900 commits. The simulator walk above was theirs too. They found what it found.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t89 text WHAT I CHECK.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t90 text I gate what lands, and I read the failures rather than the summaries. When an audit reports 48 findings, the ones I read first are the ones it closed without a fix, because a process that cannot report a negative is not a process.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t91 heading What went wrong, Value: 2\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t92 text What went wrong\n\t\t\t\t\t\t93 text A simulator walk found the flag detail sheet’s Report button rendering, looking enabled, taking the tap, and doing nothing. It is the Apple 1.2(b) objectionable-content sheet, and that screen is its only mount point in the whole app, for flags and for comments, so the report mechanism of a product built on things strangers submit was dead in every path, and would have shipped that way.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t94 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t95 text It had unit tests. They passed. They mounted the sheet on its own, and the defect was in  where the parent mounted it , which a child’s test cannot see by construction. Four comments in the code asserted the broken arrangement was proven practice, each citing another, and the one citation that pointed outside the ring pointed at a screen where that arrangement is correct for a reason that did not apply here.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t96 text The structural fix was one line: a closing tag moved from before the pair of sheets to after them. The comments were rewritten as part of the bug, because they were the reason it kept coming back. The repo had already solved this same iOS error twice, and the correct pattern was sitting three lines above the broken one in the same file. The replacement test renders the parent instead of the child, and it was checked the only way that means anything: it fails against the old arrangement.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t97 text This is the support work Flagstone demonstrates: tracing a failed interaction to its cause, choosing a repair around accessibility and privacy risks, checking the regression evidence, and deciding what is ready to release.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t98 heading Reflection, Value: 2\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t99 text Reflection\n\t\t\t\t\t\t100 text Building this reinforced something I already believed: privacy isn’t a feature you add later. It’s a foundation, especially when the people using your app are sharing vulnerable information about the barriers they face every day.\n\t\t\t\t\t101 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t102 text 2,971 TESTS PASSING\n\t\t\t\t\t\t103 link Description: MEASURED 2026-08-16, METHOD, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/work/flagstone#flagstone-test-count-method\n\t\t\t\t\t104 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t105 text Run from a fresh clone of the public repo, not a local best case: npm ci  then npx jest --ci -w 3 . That run reported 204 suites, 2,971 passing, 32 todo, 0 failing. The homepage receipt says 2,900+ because the suite grows most weeks. (Separate from the portfolio ’ s own measured 763 on the accessibility page.)\n\t\t\t\t\t106 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t107 text SKY HALISKY · OKANAGAN VALLEY, BRITISH COLUMBIA\n\t\t\t\t\t108 link Description: A Brief Account, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/about/\n\t\t\t\t\t109 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t110 text INSIDE THE BUILD\n\t\t\t\t\t\t111 heading See it in motion., Value: 2\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t112 text See it in motion.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t113 content list\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t114 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t115 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t116 container Flagstone reporting flow moving from the Explore map into the Report a flag form, through category selection and back to the updated map.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t117 button Play animation: Flagstone reporting flow moving from the Explore map into the Report a flag form, through category selection and back to the updated map.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t118 text From map pin to report form, the current reporting flow in motion. CAPTURED\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t119 text 2026-09-01\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t120 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t121 text Reporting a barrier: quick-fill templates, category, severity, and description.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t122 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t123 text Community review: neighbours verify barriers and mark resolved reports.\n\t\t\t\t\t124 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t125 text MORE WORK\n\t\t\t\t\t\t126 heading Continue reading., Value: 2\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t127 text Continue reading.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t128 content list\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t129 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t130 image The Claude Corp front door, with the fifteen-role pipeline under the editorial hero.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t131 heading Claude Corp: read the case study, Value: 3\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t132 link Description: Claude Corp: read the case study, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/work/claude-corp/\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t133 text A governed multi-agent development system I designed and operate: 15 AI roles, a written Constitution, review gates, and autonomy bounded by explicit rules.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t134 link Description: View project: Claude Corp case study, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/work/claude-corp/\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t135 link Description: Open live demo for Claude Corp (opens in new tab), Value: claudecorp.skypistudio.com/\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t136 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t137 image The Dashboard command center, showing project health, agent activity and pending decisions on one calm board.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t138 heading Claude Corp Dashboard: read the case study, Value: 3\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t139 link Description: Claude Corp Dashboard: read the case study, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/work/dashboard/\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t140 text The operator console for my agent team: what needs me, what is blocked, how each project is doing. Private in use; a public demo runs on synthetic data only.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t141 link Description: View project: Claude Corp Dashboard case study, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/work/dashboard/\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t142 link Description: Open live demo for Claude Corp Dashboard (opens in new tab), Value: dashboard.skypistudio.com/\n\t\t\t\t\t143 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t144 heading Have something like this? Write to me., Value: 2\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t145 text Have something like this? Write to me.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t146 link Description: EMAIL ME, Value: mailto:hello@skypistudio.com?subject=About%20Flagstone\n\t\t\t\t147 container\n\t\t\t\t\t148 link Description: Sky Halisky, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/\n\t\t\t\t\t149 text SMALL, EXACT SOFTWARE, DOCUMENTED HONESTLY. SKYPI STUDIO: EST. 2026\n\t\t\t\t\t150 heading SITE, Value: 2\n\t\t\t\t\t\t151 text SITE\n\t\t\t\t\t152 content list\n\t\t\t\t\t\t153 link Description: Home, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/\n\t\t\t\t\t\t154 link Description: The Work, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/work/\n\t\t\t\t\t\t155 link Description: Credentials, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/certificates/\n\t\t\t\t\t\t156 link Description: A Brief Account, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/about/\n\t\t\t\t\t\t157 link Description: Notes, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/blog/\n\t\t\t\t\t\t158 link Description: Accessibility, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/accessibility/\n\t\t\t\t\t\t159 link Description: Colophon, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/colophon/\n\t\t\t\t\t\t160 link Description: Let’s talk, Value: 127.0.0.1:4405/contact/\n\t\t\t\t\t161 heading ABOUT, Value: 2\n\t\t\t\t\t\t162 text ABOUT\n\t\t\t\t\t163 text Sky Halisky  builds small, careful AI tools. Accessible by default, useful by design, so no one’s left out. Built in public, honest about what ships: every one of them live on the open internet.\n\t\t\t\t\t164 link Description: Full history on LinkedIn (opens in new tab), Value: linkedin.com/in/skyler-halisky\n\t\t\t\t\t165 heading ELSEWHERE, Value: 2\n\t\t\t\t\t\t166 text ELSEWHERE\n\t\t\t\t\t167 content list\n\t\t\t\t\t\t168 link Description: Send email to hello@skypistudio.com, Value: mailto:hello@skypistudio.com\n\t\t\t\t\t\t169 link Description: GitHub (opens in new tab), Value: github.com/skypie99\n\t\t\t\t\t\t170 link Description: LinkedIn (opens in new tab), Value: linkedin.com/in/skyler-halisky\n\t\t\t\t\t171 text BUILT IN THE OKANAGAN VALLEY, BRITISH COLUMBIA. NO ANALYTICS. NO COOKIES.\n\t\t\t172 container Infobar Container\n\t\t\t\t173 container Infobar\n\t\t\t\t\t174 container\n\t\t\t\t\t\t175 text Continue where you left off: Chromium restores your tabs every time you restart. To turn this off, go to\n\t\t\t\t\t\t176 link Settings, opens in new tab.\n\t\t\t\t\t177 button Close\n\t\t\t178 tab group\n\t\t\t\t179 pop up button Tab Search\n\t\t\t\t180 container\n\t\t\t\t\t181 tab (selected, settable, boolean) Description: Flagstone: Sky Halisky, Value: on\n\t\t\t\t\t\t182 button Close\n\t\t\t\t183 button New Tab\n\t184 close button\n\t185 full screen button Help: this button also has an action to zoom the window, Secondary Actions: zoom the window\n\t186 minimize button\n187 menu bar\n\t188 Google Chrome for Testing\n\t189 File\n\t190 Edit\n\t191 View\n\t192 History\n\t193 Bookmarks\n\t194 Profiles\n\t195 Tab\n\t196 Window\n\t197 Help\n\nThe focused UI element is 15 HTML content Flagstone: Sky Halisky, URL: 127.0.0.1:4405/work/flagstone"